From d43e8636bf45fdb3356a40d087b1a92d7ca61dcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:50:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cloud): seal payload content on both cloud sync rails

Forward-port of fork patch 0008 onto upstream's restructured sync package.

Both rails are covered, as before: the chunks rail through
Syncer.sealChunkForCloud/openChunkFromCloud, and the mutations rail through
MutationTransport.EnableSealing. Sealing is field-level -- the server still
reads project, sync_id, session_id, type, scope, topic_key and timestamps,
because InsertMutationBatch and WriteChunk validate and index on those. Only
observation title/content, prompt content and session summary are opaque.

Upstream moved things this patch depended on:

- The cloud export path is now exportCloudMutationChunks, which splits an
  export into size-bounded parts (#833). Each part is sealed inside the loop,
  before CanonicalizeForProject and before chunkcodec.ChunkID, because the
  server rejects a chunk whose id does not hash the payload it received.
- There are now four chunk read sites, not three.
  preflightLegacyChunkOwnership is new since 47f281c and reads chunk bodies
  for ownership checks; missing it would have failed legacy imports with a
  JSON parse error on ciphertext. The other three moved and one was renamed
  (exportedRelationKeys -> exportedChunkKeys).
- ChunkData still carries Mutations, so the journal still needs sealing.
  Verified by mutation rather than by assumption: skipping chunkMutations
  fails TestCloudExportSealsTheMutationJournalToo with
  'mutations[1].payload.title went out in the clear'.
- The package imports stdlib sync as stdsync; internal/sync is itself
  package sync.

Two upstream tests needed reworking rather than fixing, because they assert
things sealing deliberately makes untrue:

- TestCloudSyncPreservesPiPromptIdentityUnderProjectScope imports into a
  second store, which now needs the key, so it gets shareCloudKey like the
  other cross-store tests.
- cloudstore's prompt delete propagation tests matched dashboard rows by
  plaintext. The dashboard cannot see plaintext in this fork and never will;
  matching on it would have made the positive assertions pass vacuously. The
  helper now seals the expected content and matches that -- deterministic
  nonces make it reproducible -- and fails loudly if plaintext ever does
  reach the dashboard. The #837 delete-propagation contract is unchanged.

Mutation-checked both directions: disabling openChunkFromCloud fails the
round trip with an encrypted title, and skipping the journal seal fails the
journal test. Full suite matches the upstream baseline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
